          },
          {
            "path": "$.units[24].text",
            "old": "The mirror image shows something else. Reflecting the graph swaps rise and run. Where the exponential has slope equal to its height, the logarithm at x has slope one over x. At one, that slope is exactly one. So the natural logarithm of one point zero one is almost exactly zero point zero one. A small percentage change becomes the same small step, which is why this base is called natural.",
            "new": "The mirror image shows something else. Reflecting the graph swaps rise and run. Where the exponential has slope equal to its height, the logarithm at x has slope one over x. At one, that slope is exactly one. So the natural logarithm of one point zero one is almost exactly zero point zero one. A small percentage change becomes the same small step, which is why base e is called natural."
          },
          {
            "path": "$.units[54].text",
            "old": "Combining the roots. Here a square root is e to the power of half a logarithm. Each minus one has principal angle pi, so the two roots together turn by pi and point at minus one. But the product, one, has principal angle zero, not two pi. Halving the lost lap leaves a half turn, and a half turn is a factor of minus one. The root of a product equals the product of the roots only up to that sign.",
            "new": "Combining the roots. Here a square root is e to the power of half a logarithm. Each minus one has principal angle pi, so the two roots together turn by pi and point at minus one. But the product, one, has principal angle zero, not two pi. Taking half of the lost lap leaves a half turn, and a half turn is a factor of minus one. The root of a product equals the product of the roots only up to that sign."
          }
        ]
